<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Makryplagi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1264">1264</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Makryplagi was fought in 1264 in a mountain pass of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> between the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> and the Byzantine army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> won, capturing the Byzantine commanders and halting the first Byzantine offensive from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a>, after the earlier Frankish victory at Prinitza.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> in 1259, the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> obtained fortresses in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> from the captured Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a>, as the price of his release. William also pledged to become Michael's vassal but renounced the oath on his return and negotiated with the Pope and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> powers for a joint effort against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. Michael sent an army of Turkish mercenaries and Greek troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> under his half-brother, the sebastokrator Constantine Palaiologos, who took much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> before a far smaller Latin force defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-prinitza">Battle of Prinitza</a>.</p><p>When Constantine resumed operations, the head of his vanguard, the megas konostaulos Michael Kantakouzenos, was killed near Sergiana in northern Elis, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a>, and Constantine turned to besiege the fortress of Nikli. There his Turkish mercenaries, over 1,000 horsemen under Melik and Shalik, demanded six months' arrears of pay. He refused, and Anselin of Toucy persuaded the two chieftains to desert to William with most of their men. Feigning illness, Constantine left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, leaving the megas domestikos Alexios Philes and the parakoimomenos John Makrenos in command.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Philes marched into Messenia and occupied the pass of Makryplagi near Gardiki Castle. William, reinforced by the Turkish contingent, attacked although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> held strong positions on the high ground. Two attacks were beaten off, but a third, made mainly by the Turkish horsemen under Anselin of Toucy, broke the Byzantines, who fled in panic. Philes, Makrenos and Alexios Kaballarios were captured with many Greek nobles. At Veligosti, when William called the defeat God's punishment on Michael for breaking his oaths, Philes answered that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> was the proper heritage of the Emperor and that it was William who had broken his oaths.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>William failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> but repopulated and fortified old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, pillaged the surrounding province and withdrew to Nikli. The fighting had devastated and depopulated his principality, and the war dwindled into skirmishes. A proposal to marry Michael's heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a> to William's daughter Isabella foundered on the opposition of the Achaean barons, and William became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> by the Treaty of Viterbo. Only in the early 14th century did the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> resume their gradual recovery of the peninsula.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Makryplagi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Franks won notable victories at Prinitza in 1263 and <b>Makryplagi</b> in 1264, but the frontier moved steadily westward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li></ul></section>
